~ core.test — the test harness: `describe`/`it`, the report they print, and what both are
~ built from. Import with `<< core.test`. Reference: docs/corelib/test/README.md.
~
~ ── Writing a suite ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
~ The module's exports are reached through its binding: `test.describe`, `test.it`, …
~
~   test.describe(name, body)  group of cases (nestable). `body` is a `() -> $` closure.
~   test.it(name, body)        one case, reported once it has run.
~
~ ── Assertions ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
~ `assert` and `expect` are provided by the COMPILER, so they need no import and are not
~ defined here. Both take the value under test and a matcher:
~
~   assert(2 + 2, equals(4))    report at YOUR call site and exit 101 on failure
~   expect(2 + 2, equals(4))    report, then end the case (tests only) — the suite
~                               carries on with the next one
~
~ The matchers are `equals`, `contains`, `not`, `isOk`, `isNotOk` — see
~ docs/corelib/test/README.md.
~
~   test.failAt(message)  fail outright at the CALLER's location, the way `assert` does —
~                         what a check of your own is built from. Take a trailing
~                         `site :: Site` and forward it to report ITS caller:
~                           assertEven = (n :: Num, site :: Site) -> $ =>
~                             n % 2 == 0 ? $ : test.failAt("`n` is odd", site)
~
~ ── The run's state, and the case lifecycle ────────────────────────────────────────
~ `quilon test` runs a file's top-level `test.describe` blocks and ends by calling
~ `reportSummary()`, whose result is the run's status — 0 passes the suite, anything else
~ fails it.
~
~ Everything the run records is reachable through these (each called `test.<name>(…)`),
~ and a suite may ask about it too:
~
~   casesPassed()  casesFailed()  nestingDepth()      what the run has recorded
~   enterSuite(name)   leaveSuite()                   open and close a group
~   caseFailing()  finishCase(name)                   ask about, then close, a case
~
~ ── The report ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
~ The lifecycle reports each event through the compiler's test registry, which renders it
~ the way `quilon test` asked (`--reporter human|json`) and skips what `--only` left out.
<< core.io

~ Report `message` at `site` — the location of the call that left the `site` argument off —
~ in the compiler's diagnostic frame, then exit 101.
>> failAt = (message :: Text, site :: Site) -> $ => <
  color = __color_enabled(io.stderr)
  position = color ? "\e[36m" : ""
  problem = color ? "\e[1;31m" : ""
  frame = color ? "\e[2m" : ""
  plain = color ? "\e[0m" : ""

  number = "`site.line`"
  gutter = " ".repeat(number.length)
  lead = " ".repeat(site.column - 1)
  carets = "^".repeat(site.width)

  ~ Keep in step with `shorten_path` in the runtime, which shortens a path the same way.
  room = 60
  file = site.file.length > room
    ? "…" + site.file.slice(site.file.length - room + 1, site.file.length)
    : site.file

  io.eprint("`position``file`:`site.line`:`site.column`:`plain`")
  io.eprint("`problem``message``plain`")
  io.eprint("`frame``gutter` |`plain`")
  io.eprint("`frame``number` |`plain` `site.excerpt`")
  io.eprint("`frame``gutter` |`plain` `lead``problem``carets``plain`")
  __exit(101)
>

~ ── The run's state ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

~ How many cases have run with no failing `expect`.
>> casesPassed = () -> Num => < __test_passed() >

~ How many cases have run with at least one failing `expect`. Non-zero is what makes a
~ run exit non-zero.
>> casesFailed = () -> Num => < __test_failed() >

~ How many `describe` groups are open: 0 outside any group, 1 inside an outermost one.
>> nestingDepth = () -> Num => < __test_depth() >

~ ── The case lifecycle ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
~ What `describe` and `it` call, in this order:
~   enterSuite(name) … the group's body … leaveSuite()
~   the case's body … finishCase(name)

~ Open the group `name`, reporting it; yields the depth it sits at (1 for an outermost
~ group).
>> enterSuite = (name :: Text) -> Num => < __test_suite_enter(name) >

~ Close the group just entered; yields the depth that remains.
>> leaveSuite = () -> Num => < __test_suite_leave() >

~ Whether the case now running has already failed an `expect`. A failing `expect` ends the
~ case right there, so this only ever answers for code that runs before one could have
~ failed.
>> caseFailing = () -> Bool => < __test_case_failing() == 1 >

~ Close the case `name` that just ran, tallying it as passed or failed and reporting it;
~ yields the depth it sits at.
>> finishCase = (name :: Text) -> Num => < __test_case_finish(name) >

~ ── The harness ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

~ A group's run: open it, run its body, close it.
runSuite = (name :: Text, body :: () -> $) -> $ => <
  enterSuite(name)
  body()
  leaveSuite()
  $
>

~ A group of cases, nestable. `body` runs immediately; the group is only there to name
~ what it contains. A group the run did not select is passed over, body and all.
>> describe = (name :: Text, body :: () -> $) -> $ => <
  __test_suite_selected(name) == 1 ? runSuite(name, body) : $
>

~ A case's run: run its body, then close it. `__test_run_case` ends `body` at its first
~ failing `expect`, however deeply that failure is nested — a lambda passed to `.each`
~ included — rather than letting the rest of it run.
runCase = (name :: Text, body :: () -> $) -> $ => <
  __test_run_case(body)
  finishCase(name)
  $
>

~ One case, reported once `body` has run. A failing `expect` inside `body` ends the case
~ right there — nothing after it runs — so the report says which way the case went and
~ the run continues with the next one. A case the run did not select is passed over.
>> it = (name :: Text, body :: () -> $) -> $ => <
  __test_case_selected(name) == 1 ? runCase(name, body) : $
>

~ The run's total, and its status — non-zero when any case failed. The synthesized entry
~ point `quilon test` runs ends with this call, so what it returns is what the suite exits
~ with.
>> reportSummary = () -> Num => < __test_summary() >
